  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The department's main river, the Argens winds between Le Muy and Roquebrune-sur-Argens before reaching the sea at Fréjus, at the foot of the Rocher de Roquebrune. The Roquebrune tourist office presents it as one of the Var's last largely wild rivers, to explore by canoe, kayak or paddleboard.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The Roquebrune-sur-Argens tourist office says swimming is allowed in the Argens, but there is no developed or supervised site: a campsite in the town specifies that no bathing site is declared there. No dog rule is published for these banks; a Pays de Fayence guide points out that, unless a municipal order is posted, riverbanks allow dogs to swim. Water level and flow vary a lot with the season, and water quality can be degraded after heavy rain: look at the signs and the town's orders.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: pick a quiet bank, keep your dog on a leash near the water and don't let it swim after a storm.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Rocher de Roquebrune, a red sandstone massif, overlooks the valley and offers lovely views to extend the outing on foot.</p>
